  {
    "episode_id": 47420,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/7/47420.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 14,
    "turn_text": "proud of the work. that I've done within my own house as a husband and father. And I always say to people, other people can be a senator, other people can be a congressman, but only one person can be the husband to my wife and the father to my children. And you're sort of the indispensable person in that regard. And I take that very, very seriously. And the opportunity you're given to form a life and put them on a path to success and to heaven. That's sort of the bottom line for me. We've had eight children. We have one of those children in heaven. We lost shortly after birth. Our youngest is a special needs little girl. She has trisome 18, which is a very serious disability. We've been blessed now for 13 years to be able to care for her and continue to care for her and well for the rest of her life. And, you know, that's, some people look at that as a burden. We, we, we, we, and this family have recognized the incredible blessing that she brings to us. And we even wrote a book about it. Her name is Bella. We could, my wife and I wrote a book called Bella's Gift. And the rest of our children are doing great. They're thankfully healthy and We have three that are married. We have one getting married in two weeks. You know, if you ever saw the movie, Follow the Bride, I'm going through a Steve Martin, Father of the Bride experience right now because the wedding receptions in our backyard. So we've watched that movie several times this year. And everyone says, that's you, Dad. You're him. It's been an exciting year. So the family's great. You feel very, very blessed. And maybe it's from my own experiences by family growing up. And also this family that I'm in right now is how central the family is to the vast majority of people's lives and how important it is for raising the next generation. I wrote a book back in 2004 called It Takes a Family. It was in response to Hillary Clinton's book, It Takes a Village. Obviously, a village is important, but at the essential element that I think has been fractured and hurting in America today is the family. I made the call for conservatives to start putting family policy up where we put all the other things that conservatives tend to agree with, which is limited government and pro-growth and all these sorts of things. What about doing things to strengthen not just the economy, but the family? We tend to give it short shrift as conservatives. We talk about it. We value it. But we don't really do anything in our public lives to try to support it. And so I sort of made the case that we need to look at public policy ideas that can support a changing family dynamic in America. We're not the same country as we were 50 years ago from the standpoint of the family. and policies that may have worked 50 years ago aren't working today and we need to do something about it.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_02",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
