    "episode_id": 47420,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/7/47420.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 17,
    "turn_text": "And it made sense. Some of the numbers that I looked at, it was like 48% of these folks that are that are going back that have to go back to work or they don't have the resources will end up on public assistance. So there we're going to end up paying, whether it's on food stamps or other things, they're going to end up paying for that. Forget the value that we're talking about to that family. I can't forget that. That's a wonderful thing. But to those that are just sort of hardened business folks saying, look, we need to see a return on our investment. There's a huge return on our investment by giving an alternative resource for them. And I believe that a real bipartisan effort that I believe is taking place You know, and just after taking the family leave act or taking paid leave, mothers are 39% less likely to go on public assistance and 40% less likely to go on food stamps. There's your ROI for just the folks that are looking at economics of it. But then the long term benefits for that child and for that family, you know, I've started wax philosophical with you. But to talk, Bill, when he comes to America and he says America's great because in essence, we are Good people. That's that was his thing. America's good. And what he meant by that is it's it's churches, it's communities, it's families.",
