    "turn_idx": 19,
    "turn_text": "other. Yeah, we and we had a loving compassionate side. I don't think that's ever government's role to be the love agent. But I do believe they can get out of the way or they can use other resources to be able to support that. And I think that's what this is doing. I believe this is a wonderful tool. And again, it's bipartisan. It's not a philosophical. You're going to want to jump into some of the divisive pools that are out there that we could be. But it's something that we can all be very supportive of. And it doesn't, you can do this without breaking the bank. Again, if you put it so that it's just on small businesses, six weeks, two months, three months, six months for a small business to pay for this, in many cases would make it economically unfeasible or unlikely to take place. And so I think that there will be some nuances to the legislation, I'm sure. But I believe this is a really strong pattern of what we're working on. I know that our organization is made up of a lot of not-for-profit organizations. We have what we call community-based care. That's why I think Florida strategically aligned to do a lot of this type of work and be in support of. And we have an area that we're working on economic self-sufficiency. So we do not want you to be on the government dole. It should be there to help you during times of crisis. That is a goodness piece. But on the other hand, what's really good is to get them so they can be economically self-sufficient. And I think this is charging right down that path. Well, give me an idea. I saw your legislative packet on how to use a great tool kit that was put together. give our audience a sort of an idea of how they could engage in their own community. By the way, we're talking particularly here in Florida, but this will go across the United States. In fact, we got international folks watching our our nurturing well-being with Kurt Kelly. So tell us how we could get involved in this.",
