  {
    "episode_id": 47420,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/7/47420.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 20,
    "turn_text": "Well, you know, there are we're talking about federal legislation here. I think It would be most appropriate to do something like this on the federal level. And the reason is because, as I said before, there are existing programs. The two approaches that I've seen so far and there may be more that are coming is to use existing government programs and give people options as to when to receive the benefits from these programs that they pay for in many cases. One is the Social Security Program, send a Rubio's an advocate of this to take payments that you would get upon your retirement and delay your retirement by, let's say you want to take two months to spend with your child after the birth or adoption of a child. So you would take two months of your Social Security payments that you'd be getting 30 or 40 years from hence and you would delay your retirement by, if you take two months, you would delay it by four months. You say, well, that's not fair. I'm getting two months, but I'm delaying four months. Yeah, but you're getting that money now, not 30 years from now. So it's the time value of money. So again, most people would say, you know, instead of hitting retirement at 67, I would be 67 to four months, but I get to spend the first two months of my life, of my child's life, you know, with her. That to me is, you know, those are choices. Now, people want to say, I don't want to do that. Okay, don't. But to give people the option to do that, to give people the flexibility to manage their own lives. I mean, that's one of the things that I like about these approaches, whether it's the Social Security approach, Whether it's as I mentioned before, the child tax credit approach where you instead of taking your $2,500 child tax credit for the next five years, you reduce your child tax credit by $500 over the next five years, and you take $2,500 and you use it now so you can spend a month or two after the birth or adoption of your child to care for that. Again, you don't have to do it, but you have the option to do it. And to me, this is no new money, no new taxes. It's simply taking resources that you would be getting through payments in Social Security, things that you paid into, or in the case of the CHOP credit, government program that's in place to help families to begin with, and giving families the flexibility to use it when they believe it's best for them and their family. I don't know why anyone would object to that. It doesn't make any sense to me that people would object to that. Hopefully, we can build support for that by your network and others contacting their senators, both Republican and Democrats. Again, some of these, not all of them, some of them are bipartisan bills and try to get support for these ideas.",
